      <!-- FAQ ACCORDION SECTION -->
      <h3 style="font-size: 1.5rem; font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
        Frequently Asked Questions: Spectrophotometry &amp; Chemical Kinetics
      </h3>

      <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What is the formal definition and how do you state beer lambert law?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            The beer lambert law states that the optical absorbance of a solution is directly proportional to the concentration of the absorbing species and the optical pathlength through the sample: A = ε · b · c.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What are the standard beer lambert law units for molar absorptivity?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            In the beer lambert equation, absorbance (A) is dimensionless, pathlength (b) is in centimeters (cm), concentration (c) is in molarity (mol/L), resulting in molar absorptivity (ε) units of L·mol⁻¹·cm⁻¹.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How do you perform a beer lambert law calculation example with a standard curve?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Using ordinary least-squares regression on multi-point standard solutions (A = m·c + c₀), unknown sample concentrations are interpolated via c_unk = (A_unk - c₀) / m.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">Why is the absorption law in uv spectroscopy subject to deviations at high concentrations?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Above 0.01 M, inter-chromophore distances shrink below 10 nm. Neighboring electrostatic fields perturb electron distributions, altering molar absorptivity (ε). Furthermore, high optical densities let monochromator stray light dominate the detector signal.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">How does the studio model methylene blue photodegradation and dye remediation?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            By monitoring chromophore absorbance decay over irradiation time, the reaction kinetics engine fits experimental data to pseudo-first-order rate equations (ln(C₀ / Ct) = k₁ · t) to evaluate photodegradation efficiency and half-life.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details style="background: #ffffff; border: 1.5px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 10px; padding: 1.15rem;">
          <summary style="font-weight: 800; color: #0f172a; cursor: pointer; font-size: 0.95rem;">What are the core principles of chemical kinetics chemistry when extracting rate constants?</summary>
          <p style="margin: 0.65rem 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: #334155; line-height: 1.7;">
            Chemical kinetics chemistry evaluates the time-dependent velocity of chemical reactions. In spectrophotometric degradation assays, linearizing reactant concentration decay allows precise extraction of apparent rate constants and catalyst performance.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

